  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level trough responsible for the cooler airmass over the \nregion will pivot east into New England on Thursday. Meanwhile \nhigh pressure will expand at the surface across the Central \nGreat Lakes maintaining cool and dry conditions to end the week.\nOvernight lows through Thursday night will be in the low 50s \nand high temperatures will gradually warm 3-5 degrees each day \nthrough Saturday. A healthy cumulus deck of clouds is expected \ninland again on Thursday but will tend to clear late afternoon. \nMostly sunny to partly cloudy conditions continue through \nSaturday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nActive weather will return Sunday into Monday as the upper \nlevel ridge expands eastward into the Central Great Lakes \naccompanied by the moisture and instability axis. Dewpoints \nreturn to the mid 60s with ML CAPE values of 1000-2000 J/kg on \nSunday. A shortwave moving through the northwest flow aloft will\nlikely provide a focus for scattered thunderstorms and \nsufficient shear is expected for some storms to be strong. By \nMonday a warm front will be in the vicinity and likely act as a \nfocus for additional thunderstorm activity. Given uncertainty in\nplacement and coverage will keep most areas at scattered \ncoverage for now but could trend up once we gain confidence in \ntiming. Temperatures will trend back to more summer like \nconditions with highs in the mid to upper 80s and increasing \nhumidity to start the week.",
